      "excerpt": "@akimfomin The PR's test already covers that negative case. In `old_state_reset_does_not_free_a_replacement_heaps_dynamic_slots`, the old State holds heap A's range `(first, 1)`. After A is destroyed and B takes the same address, B allocates the same `(first, 1)` into a new State, then every other slot is filled. The stale `old.reset` must then leave B's allocator full (`dynamic(1) == None`). Only",
